From 0851218c336e88146a3688a793e9c34e7137eb68 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:01:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add the multigrid reduction of hypre (HypreMGR)

The multigrid reduction of hypre (MGR) is a preconditioner of the
factories: one cycle from a null initial guess, the unknowns being split
by their components, given to hypre by markers whatever their ordering
(HYPRE_MGRSetCpointsByPointMarkerArray). Its options are the components
kept at each level of reduction (CoarseComponents: a list, or a list of
lists, each level keeping a non empty strict subset of the components of
the previous one; by default, one level keeping all the components but
the last one), the F-relaxation, its number of sweeps, the interpolation
and the restriction, and the verbosity level. The preconditioner of
hypre is created again, with the settings, for each new operator.

The values of the options are the ones documented by hypre 3.2.0 and,
when hypre computes on a GPU, the ones it computes there: the other ones
apply the relaxations, interpolations and restrictions of the host to
the matrices on the GPU and stop the program, and the V-cycle relaxation
frees memory which is not its own.

HypreMGRTest checks it on a cantilever beam in linear elasticity, the
unknowns ordered by nodes and by vector dimension: with the Jacobi
F-relaxation of hypre (default), which is slower than the Jacobi
preconditioner on this problem, and with an F-relaxation by algebraic
multigrid, with which the GMRES solver of MFEM needs about 35 iterations
on the host and 50 on a GPU, against 150 to 300 with Jacobi; one and two
levels, the GMRES solver of hypre, a second operator, and the refusals.
Markers computed for the wrong ordering are detected (66 iterations
instead of 34). In single precision, two levels let GMRES report its
convergence with large residuals: only one level is checked. The generic
test runs it on the linear elasticity with the F-relaxation by algebraic
multigrid, and skips it for the scalar unknowns.
